<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bronze Age</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Age is the period in which bronze was the dominant material for tools and weapons, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> lasting from about the late fourth millennium to around 1200 BCE. Its late phase saw a system of great powers including the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>. It ended in the widespread collapse of the twelfth century BCE, after which Phoenician cities, ancestors of Punic civilisation, rose to prominence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bronze-age.jpg" alt="Bronze Age" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Want · CC BY-SA 3.0 cz · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metallurgical_diffusion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Bronze Age is an archaeological term for a phase in the development of material culture among ancient societies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A culture belongs to it if it produced bronze by smelting its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and alloying it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, arsenic or other metals, or traded for bronze from producing areas elsewhere. It is the middle period of the three-age system, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a> transition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. It began at different times in different places, but had begun in much of the Old World by 3000 BCE.</p><p>In a region the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> or of writing, whichever came first, is usually taken to mark the end of the Bronze Age. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> use a five-period system without one, though the Moche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> developed bronze smelting independently. In much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a> iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">copper smelting</a> appeared at about the same time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> entered the Bronze Age first, with the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in the mid-4th millennium BCE. Cultures there practised intensive agriculture, developed writing, invented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potters-wheel">potter's wheel</a>, and created centralised governments, written law codes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">Cuneiform</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and hieroglyphs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> are the earliest practical writing systems. Egypt's Bronze Age began around 3150 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> about 3300 BCE. The Near Eastern late period, from about 1550 to 1200 BCE, ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a>; the Hittite kingdom disintegrated after 1180 BCE amid turmoil conjecturally linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> was harder and more durable than other metals then available. Iron needs about 1,250 °C to smelt and stayed out of common use until the end of the 2nd millennium BCE, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> melt at lower temperatures that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> pottery kilns could reach. Yet in many areas bronze remained rare and expensive, chiefly because tin occurs in few places. Some societies used it only for weapons or elite art, such as Chinese ritual bronzes, while ordinary farmers still used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> network imported tin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where copper was mined and alloyed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bronze Age” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Byblos was the main point of contact between Egypt and the Levant for much of the <b>Bronze Age</b>, and through its harbour flowed timber, ideas and artistic styles in both directions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was inhabited from the Neolithic, and a <b>Bronze Age</b> settlement at Emporio on its south coast had contacts with the Minoan and Mycenaean worlds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dilmun, centred on the island of Bahrain, was the hub of the <b>Bronze Age</b> sea trade linking Mesopotamia with Magan and the Indus, a merchant state with its own seals and weights whose tens of thousands of burial mounds survive and whose name became a byword for paradise…</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ebla was one of the earliest literate states outside Mesopotamia; the archive of its burned palace revealed a Semitic-speaking kingdom of the twenty-fourth century BCE with its own textile economy, treaties and diplomacy, and overturned the idea that early <b>Bronze Age</b> Syria was merely a periphery of Sumer.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian sources mention the land in the late <b>Bronze Age</b>: the list of the pharaoh Seti I from c. 1215 BCE, the chronicle of a campaign by Ramesses III, and a letter from a scribe at a border fortress in the Wadi Tumilat under Merneptah reporting &quot;shasu-tribes of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's final military test came under Ramesses III, who repelled land and sea invasions by the Sea Peoples around 1177 BCE, preserving Egypt while almost every other <b>Bronze Age</b> power collapsed or contracted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
